SUMMARY:Drawing in Joy: Laughing with Life\, a Dance on Paper
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T200000
UID:260324AceWX0TDLEyIrvcic-2EWw
DESCRIPTION:Schedule\n\nThursdays\, April 9 – 30\, 2026 5:30 PM – 8:00
  PM\n\nLocation\n\nRoom 208\n\nExperience Level\n\nAll Levels\n\nEvent Cat
 egories: Drawing & Painting\, Extended Classes\n\nAge\\(s\\)\n\n18 and old
 er\n\nRegistration Cost:\n\n$175\n\nMaterials Fee:\n\nSee Required List\n\
 nTotal Registration Cost:\n\n$175\n\nExpress joy through drawing. Build sk
 ills in sketching\, composition & landscapes. Boost focus\, creativity\, c
 onnection & well-being.\n\nAbout the Class\n\nWhen we feel a sense of joy\
 , it is natural to express that feeling from our body. We may use our voic
 e and sing\, we may dance\, and we may use our hands in creative expressio
 n. By drawing we leave a mark of that expression. This course is shaped wi
 th skill building exercises to improve eye-hand coordination with quick sk
 etching and gesture drawing and practices of learning to see how to abstra
 ct shapes of light and dark for developing strong compositions. We will fo
 cus on landscape imagery from photos and drawing from setups in the studio
 . Drawing outside of class will be encouraged but not required. There are 
 many health benefits from drawing such as: improvements in cognitive focus
 \, freedom of expression with your hands\, the satisfaction that comes fro
 m seeing the world in a different way\, the social camaraderie of acceptan
 ce in the studio\, and the joy of doing something with your hands.\n\nRequ
 ired Materials List\n\nA good size sketch book for multimedia\n\nNewsprint
  drawing pad \\(12” x 18” or 18” x 24”\\)\n\nBlack pencils\, 6B so
 ft\n\nCompressed charcoal sticks\, black and white\n\nEraser\, kneaded rub
 ber\n\nEraser\, white vinyl\n\nBlack felt pens
LOCATION:Sonoma Community Center
URL:https://sonomacommunitycenter.org/programs/drawing-in-joy/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Small Wonders
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T170000
UID:2603248MVZiVqNy0aNLi926_uNPg
DESCRIPTION:Call for Art: Small Wonders\n\nSome of the most exquisite beau
 ty can be captured in the smallest of images and objects: the iridescence 
 of a hummingbird in flight\, a millefiori paperweight\, Faberge eggs\, or 
 a miniature woven basket. These pieces are modest in size but powerful in 
 creativity and expression.\n\nHealdsburg Center for the Arts (HCA) invites
  artists to submit paintings\, original fine art prints\, collage\, mixed 
 media\, drawings\, fiber arts\, sculptures\, ceramics\, and jewelry for th
 is exhibition.\n\nApplication Requirements:\n\n\\- Platform: All applicati
 ons must be submitted via EntryThingy\n\n\\- Eligibility: Work must be ori
 ginal and completed within the past two years. Photos in the application m
 ust represent the actual work intended for exhibition.\n\n\\- Size/Weight:
  Artwork must not exceed 12" x 12" unframed (144 sq in) or 196 sq in frame
 d. Maximum weight is 40 lbs.\n\n\\- Display: 2-D pieces must be securely w
 ired (no alligator clips). 3-D work must be stable\; artists are responsib
 le for on-site assembly if required.\n\n\\- Sales: All work must be for sa
 le. HCA retains a 40% commission\; artists receive 60%. Please list retail
  prices only.\n\nKey Dates:\n\n\\- Entry Deadline: May 10\, 2026 (9 PM)\n\
 n\\- Notification of Selection: By May 13\, 2026\n\n\\- Shipped Work Deadl
 ine: Wednesday\, May 27\, 2026 (must include pre-paid return label)\n\n\\-
  Hand-Delivery: Sunday\, May 31 (12–4 PM) and Monday\, June 1 (11 AM–1
  PM)\n\n\\- Exhibition Dates: June 4\, 2026 – July 19\, 2026\n\n\\- Open
 ing Reception: Saturday\, June 6\, 2026 (4–6 PM)\n\n\\- Pick-up/Return S
 hipping: July 19 (12–4 PM) and July 20 (11 AM–1 PM)\; shipping the wee
 k of July 20.\n\nEntry Fees:\n\n\\- Non-Members: $50 (1 entry)\, $55 (2 en
 tries)\, $60 (3 entries). Includes a $60 yearly supporter membership.\n\n\
 \- Current Members: $40 (1 entry)\, $45 (2 entries)\, $50 (3 entries).
LOCATION:Healdsburg Center for the Arts
URL:https://healdsburgcenterforthearts.org/small-wonders-2/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:What About Age? Art Exhibit
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T180000
UID:260318naTw86vJyEC0T8hl_fLg5Q
DESCRIPTION:*What About Age?* art exhibit has arrived in Marin County! Or
 iginally displayed at NYU’s Kimmel Windows\, this exhibit explores ageis
 m through contemporary art\, featuring works by both local youth and senio
 r artists.\n\n*What About Age?* is an art exhibit and educational initiat
 ive designed to challenge stereotypes about aging\, address ageism and soc
 ial isolation\, and promote intergenerational dialogue through the lens of
  art.\n\nIn partnership with Youth in Arts\, the exhibit features artworks
  by local youth and senior artists\, showcasing how age intersects with cr
 eativity\, identity\, and experience. Under the guidance of Youth in Arts\
 , these emerging and established artists work side by side to create colla
 borative pieces\, fostering meaningful connections between generations and
  reflecting the exhibit’s core message: art is ageless.\n\nThe exhibit a
 lso includes artwork panels by New York-based writer and illustrator Aubre
 y Hirsch\, first displayed at NYU’s Kimmel Windows and originally organi
 zed by Stacey Gordon\, NYU’s Program Director of Next Phase Adult Caregi
 ving and Retirement at Work Life.\n\nThe new exhibition will be on view at
  the Marin County Civic Center galleries from January 26 to May 6\, 2026. 
 Works will be displayed on the first and third floor exhibits\, Monday to 
 Friday from 9 a.m. to 6 p.m.\n\nThe *What About Age?* art exhibit is prese
 nted by Marin Cultural Association in partnership with Marin Community Fou
 ndation and Youth in Arts. Beyond the art exhibit\, the *What About Age?* 
 initiative offers programming\, led by Vivalon\, designed to challenge age
 ism and provide resources for Marin County's growing aging population.
LOCATION:Marin County Civic Center
URL:https://www.marincultural.org/exhibitions
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: The Poetic Landscape
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T180000
UID:260318lRgzE-4e8EiEm73KXdiEpg
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  3/13/26\n\n**APRIL EXHIBIT: THE 
 POETIC LANDSCAPE**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES:  March 30  – April 26\, 2026\n\n*
 *RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, APRIL 4\, 2026\, 3-5 pm: Join us to meet KAREN MILL
 ER and PHYLLIS RAPP\;  celebrate the show and enjoy a poetry reading by f
 eatured guest\, Linda Loveland Reid\, gallery artist and published author.
   Be sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthly drawing for a beau
 tiful work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSonoma
  County artist\, **Karen Miller\,** presents **The Poetic Landscape\,** pa
 stel paintings that convey Miller’s personal experience in connecting wi
 th the intrinsic beauty of natural places. In these pieces\, color\, fluid
  shapes and rhythmic design combine to create an uplifting mood – a true
  poetic interlude.  Evoking a happy mood\, “Geyserville Sunflowers\,”
  turning to the sun by nature\, joyfully dance in its luminescence. By the
  river’s edge\, concentric reflections conjure a calm sense of wellbeing
  in “Fetch.”  The viewer feels welcomed by a warm embrace entering a 
 shaded secret spot in “September Trellis.”  Focused on local scenes\,
  these works truly hone in on the stunning landscape out our doors.  Mill
 er remarked “ As I contemplate the very real places of beauty surroundin
 g us\, I try to express my painterly response in hopes it will engender re
 cognition by the viewers.” \n\nMeet the artist: Miller will be hosting 
 the Gallery on March 30\, April 5 & 15 from 11am to 2:30 pm.\n\n The Stai
 rway Smallworks Showcase features **Phyllis Rapp’s Magic Moments\,** a c
 ollection of watercolors that create an atmosphere that is both introspect
 ive and magical.  Familiar domestic scenes are transformed as humans inte
 ract with animals and birds in floral settings.  In “Silent Watcher” 
 the red-headed young woman’s green eyes lock onto ours as she stares ahe
 ad\, flanked by her avian companion.  Experiencing these works is a like 
 taking a mini-step on the wild side\, an exciting and fun experience.\n\nM
 eet the artist: Rapp will be hosting the Gallery on March 30 and April 10 
 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\nAlso join us for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG**\, Thursday\
 , April 2\, 5 – 6 pm.  The theme for the 2026 kickoff event is Bouquets
  to Art.  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the 1st Thursday of Every Month\, is
  a community event celebrating art and creativity with over 20 galleries p
 articipating.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\nUpstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Cente
 r Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 431-4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 
 pm.    **www.upstairsart gallery.net** \n\nFor further information: Con
 tact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bvdrio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Encaustic + Mixed Media Open Studio
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T210000
UID:260226Lsm2Gpefc0uvFSZMoHPPhA
DESCRIPTION:Join us at Encaustic Open Studio\, a monthly workshop designed
  for you to refine your encaustic painting skills under the guidance of k
 nowledgeable instructor Susan Heeringa Piper. Whether you're a beginner o
 r an experienced artist\, this is the perfect opportunity to immerse yours
 elf in the captivating world of encaustic art.\n\nEncaustic painting is an
  ancient technique that involves using molten beeswax and pigments to crea
 te stunning\, textured masterpieces. This unique medium allows for endless
  possibilities\, as it can be layered\, carved\, scraped\, and fused to cr
 eate mesmerizing effects and depth. From ethereal landscapes to abstract c
 reations\, encaustic painting offers a truly immersive and tactile experie
 nce. \n\nBring your own substrate or project to work on\, or start someth
 ing new!
LOCATION:Art Escape
URL:https://bohemian.com/events-calendar/#/details/encaustic-mixed-media-o
 pen-studio/18120582/2026-04-23T18
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jaimee Harris & Amber Rubarth
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T000000
UID:260224wHx3gXpaYUWgBhJjvG4TZg
DESCRIPTION:Jaimee Harris turned 30 during the pandemic. It’s a mileston
 e that is a rite of passage even during normal times. But for this Texas-b
 orn singer-songwriter\, it came in the midst of one of the strangest and m
 ost tumultuous periods in American history. When the world stopped during 
 lockdown\, Harris\, like many others\, found herself gazing back into the 
 past\, ruminating on the nature of her hometown and family origins\, and r
 eckoning with their imprint on her. The term ‘nostalgia’ derives from 
 the Greek words nostos \\(return\\) and algos \\(pain\\)\, and if Harris
 ’s Boomerang Town can be regarded as a nostalgic album\, it is only nost
 algic in the sense that the longing for home is a desire to return to the 
 past and heal old wounds.\n\n“I’m at an age where I’m wrestling with
  trying to understand the nature of my family\,” Harris says. “There
 ’s been suicide\, suicide ideation\, and there’s certainly been addict
 ion all through my family. My dad’s father died of suicide when he was 2
 5 and I was 5. I couldn’t imagine not having my dad right now.”\n\nHar
 ris’s sophomore effort\, Boomerang Town marks a bold step forward for th
 is country-folk-leaning singer-songwriter. It is an arresting\, ambitious 
 song-cycle that explores the generational arc of family\, the stranglehold
  of addiction\, and the fragile ties that bind us together as Americans.\n
 \nFor Harris\, the album began gestating around 2016\, a time of great los
 s for many in the Americana community\, with the songwriter losing several
  musicians close to her. The shift in the nation’s political landscape h
 ad ushered in a new level of polarization that saw whole swaths of cultura
 l life being demonized. For someone who grew up in a small town outside of
  Waco\, Harris believed the values instilled in her by her parents were no
 t entirely in line with how many on the left were viewing — and vilifyin
 g — Christians\, citing them as responsible for the new change in leader
 ship. As a person in recovery\, Harris has had to re-evaluate her own conn
 ection to faith and find strength in a higher power \\(“Though he’s no
 t necessarily a blue-eyed Jesus\,” she laughs\\)\, though she certainly 
 knows what it’s like to “be told how to vote” in a Southern church s
 etting. \n\nIt was from the intersection of these social\, personal\, and 
 political currents the album was born. And while much of the material on B
 oomerang Town was inspired by personal experience\, the songs on this coll
 ection are far from autobiographical xeroxed copies. More than anything\, 
 they come from a place of emotional truth.\n\nBoomerang Town traces the fo
 rtunes of a host of characters who live on the knife’s edge between hope
  and despair. The title track\, whose sound recalls the best of Mary Chapi
 n Carpenter’s ’90s work\, features a young couple from a small-town wo
 rking dead-end jobs who get “knocked up” and have their dreams put on 
 hold. It is a portrait of rural desperation and the restless search for sa
 lvation against long odds. “This is what it’s like to be a part of the
  post- “‘Born To Run’ Generation\,” Harris quips. “Springsteen
 ’s generation had somewhere to run to. I’m not so sure mine does.” F
 or the characters in these songs\, escape isn’t always a matter of geogr
 aphical distance.\n\n“I tried a lot of perspectives \\[on this one\\]\,
 ” Harris says about writing the title track. “My parents are high-scho
 ol sweethearts and I was an accident and they’re still happily married. 
 I worked at Wal-Mart when I was 19. I reflected on this guy who was the br
 other of a good friend of mine. He didn’t drop out. He knocked up his gi
 rlfriend and went into the military. Certainly \\[the song\\] is a combina
 tion of me and not me. It was me thinking about what might have gone diffe
 rently for my parents\, who are still in Waco and own a business there.”
  \n\nHarris’s father\, whom she counts as a big supporter and responsibl
 e for much of her musical education\, took her to the first Austin City Li
 mits Music Festival\, where she had the life-changing\, Eureka moment of s
 eeing Emmylou Harris\, Patty Griffin\, and Buddy and Julie Miller perform 
 on stage at the same time. It was then the young Harris knew what she had 
 to do. She had found her ticket out. \n\nHarris continues: “Why was I ab
 le to get out of my boomerang town? Why are others stuck there\, longing t
 o leave but unable to find their way out? Writing these songs\, bringing t
 hese narrators to life\, brought me closer to the answers\,” she says.\n
 \nThemes of grief and addiction permeate other sections of the record. “
 How Could You Be Gone\,” which Harris wrote with her partner\, the vener
 able folk songwriter Mary Gauthier\, reflects on the passing of a close fr
 iend during the pandemic\, as well as the 2017 death of Harris’s mentor 
 and compadre Jimmy LaFave\, a long-time fixture on the Americana scene who
  succumbed to cancer. “It’s been my experience that grief operates on 
 its own timeline\,” Harris says. “I wanted this track to build and rep
 eat with intensity to mirror the experience of relentless grief.” Anothe
 r song\, “Fall \\(Devin’s Song\\)\,” is about a former childhood cla
 ssmate of Harris’s who was accidentally shot and killed in the sixth gra
 de. The song was inspired by a series of “In Memoriam” pieces the boy
 ’s mother wrote to the local paper\, and the song serves as a tribute to
  both of them\, as well as a commentary on the timeless nature of grief. \
 n\nOne of the album’s standout tracks is the lilting\, Irish-influenced 
 “The Fair And Dark Haired Lad\,” a Chicks type-number that grapples wi
 th the seductive nature of alcohol. Another tune that deals with the demon
  rum\, “Sam’s\,” is far more dirge-like\, and its dark\, circular me
 lody mirrors the claustrophobia and sense of trapping that comes with the 
 onset of addiction and mental collapse. \n\nBoomerang Town is not entirely
  a lament\, however\, with songs like “Love is Gonna Come Again” and t
 he wistful “Missing Someone” shining with hope in the face of the dark
 ness. For this is a record that understands that love and grief are two si
 des of the same coin. It also announces the arrival of a great new songwri
 ter on the scene. \n\n“My goal is to just write the best possible song I
  can write\,” Harris says\, “and I wanted to have ten songs that made 
 sense together sonically. I still believe in the album format\, and I want
 ed to lay the groundwork as a solid songwriter.” On Boomerang Town\, Jai
 mee Harris\, who was able to find her way out — unlike so many others 
 — has accomplished all that\, and much more.
LOCATION:HopMonk Tavern Novato
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/1037151395?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=2
 87&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Joe Kaplow & Cactus Lee
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260423T000000
UID:2602242uUSBFxIwEuK-KZQkIOYww
DESCRIPTION:Before I started my career I had a choice: to take over my par
 ent's thoroughbred farm in New Jersey or pursue music full time in Califor
 nia and relinquish the farm to sale.  I still wonder what life would have 
 looked like had I stayed\, but who can resist the gold rush?  Besides\, I 
 plan to get back there one day on another farm with some other red barn. \
 n\n  \n\nMy music comes from life’s moments -- smelling 4 acres of fresh
 ly cut grass\, watching the steam from a horse's breath in the early morni
 ng\, finally holding the neck of my guitar after holding bridles and worn 
 wooden handles all day.  4000 acres of freshly burnt wildfire\, watching t
 he steam from the Pacific Ocean's breath in the early morning\, finally ho
 lding the neck of my guitar after clutching the worn steering wheel of the
  tour van all day.  \n\nNow my reality is in Bonny Doon\, CA\, where mount
 ains meet the sea. I am in my recording studio\, aptly named Hippie Cowboy
 \, every day.  Slow and steady\; keep it fun\, stay engaged.  All for the 
 moment when the song finally sounds right.  My dad passed away a couple ye
 ars ago -- his bullwhip is on the wall\, his Harley is in the driveway. He
  visits me in dreams\, and oh\, the moment I wake up!  Sometimes I write f
 or him. Sometimes for my mom\, who I hope will move out to California soon
 .   Maybe she could volunteer at an equestrian center... \n\nA person is m
 ade from their moments\, like their music is made.  I know this is a bio b
 ut I shouldn't have to say much.  Listen to the record.  If I've done my j
 ob well\, you will know me by the end.
LOCATION:Little Saint
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/1037939564?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=2
 87&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
