BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:John Wesley Harding & Wesley Stace
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260508T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260508T000000
UID:260327zSdbDXEPOEOlwAmckPavfg
DESCRIPTION:John Wesley Harding \\(b. 22 October 1965\\) is a folk/pop sin
 ger-songwriter who has called his style of music &quot\;folk noir&quot\; a
 nd &quot\;gangsta folk&quot\;.\n\n \n\n He was born Wesley Stace in Hastin
 gs\, East Sussex\, England. His given name\, Wesley\, comes from John Wesl
 ey\, the founder of Methodism\, who preached one of his last sermons in th
 e town where Harding was born.\n\n \n\n His stage name may be seen as an a
 lmost inevitable nickname for a folk-pop musician\, in that his first name
  overlaps with the Bob Dylan album \\(and song\\) &quot\;John Wesley Hardi
 ng&quot\;. The American gunfighting historical figure John Wesley Hardin w
 as largely forgotten before 1965\, when Johnny Cash wrote and recorded the
  song &quot\;Hardin Wouldn't Run&quot\;.\n\n \n\n His education included t
 he boarding schools St. Andrews School \\(Pangbourne\, Berkshire\\)\; Milb
 ourne Lodge \\(Claygate\, Surrey\\)\; The King's School \\(Canterbury\, Ke
 nt\\)\; and university at Jesus College\, Cambridge. He left Cambridge wit
 h a First in English Literature\, but left before completing his Ph.d. in 
 Social and Political Science.\n\n \n\n Influences on him include Bob Dylan
 \, Bruce Springsteen\, Ray Davies\, and Phil Ochs. He has been frequently 
 compared to Elvis Costello probably fueled\, in part\, by the fact that tw
 o members of his early band The Good Liars \\(Pete and Bruce Thomas\\) had
  been members of The Attractions. Harding was chosen by Bruce Springsteen 
 as his first opening act in 20 years for his solo shows at the Berkeley Co
 mmunity Theatre in 1995. Harding
LOCATION:HopMonk Tavern Novato
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/1037339141?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=2
 87&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
